---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Weft — A Shared Persistent Memory For AI Agents"
  description="Have your agent remember where you left off last session without wasting context. MIT Licensed & Local First"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Memory For Your Agents</p>
      <h1>Weft Remembers What You Tell It To</h1>
      <p class="stance">
        Weft is a persistent brain you share with your agents that only remembers what you tell it
        to. You decide what's important for agents to pick up between conversations; Weft adds
        provenance and context so that those memories are just a semantic search away.
      </p>
      <p class="stance">
        Weft doesn't remember everything on purpose. You don't need your agent to remember every
        time you asked "Does that sound right to you?" You want something that remembers what you
        decided. Weft prioritizes predictability and trust over maximum recall.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/getting-started">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft" rel="me noreferrer">Read the source</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-art" aria-hidden="true">
        <!-- Interlace mark: indigo weft over slate warp, theme-aware via CSS vars -->
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 100 100" fill="none">
          <path d="M80,24 L64,77 L43,34" stroke="var(--warp)" stroke-width="14" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
          <path d="M20,24 L36,77 L57,34" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="14" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="what-is-weft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Why Did I Make Weft?</h2>
      <p>
        If you want to send information to your agent in a new session, without a memory system,
        you have two choices:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>You can copy and paste a handoff each time you hit <code>clear</code></li>
        <li>You try to save stuff to a handoff file and tell your agent to update and import it each run.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This works, until it doesn't.</p>
      <p>
        Your memories are locked in that repo or platform, and if you switch to something else,
        that new agent might not be able to read them. Hunting for a specific memory? Your agent
        uses <code>grep</code>, and if it finds it, you'll end up loading a ton of context that
        you don't care about.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every session starts from zero and you waste time trying to get the agent up to speed
        until the real work begins.
      </p>
      <p>Weft helps fix that.</p>
      <p>It's a private, local-first platform that treats your memories as a first-class data system.</p>
      <p>
        When you end a session, Weft lets your agent hand off a summary of what they did and what
        comes next. In that new session your agent can prime itself with just the information it
        needs to get started.
      </p>
      <p>
        Memories are stored in a database that allows for semantic search accessible by any agent
        connected to your Weft MCP.
      </p>
      <p>Same agent, different agent, <em>different machine</em> — it doesn't matter.</p>
      <p>Your memories belong to you, not your harness.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What's In A Weft Memory?</h2>
      <p>
        You have a breakthrough in how you work with your agent and say "Make this a rule:
        Whenever we push a PR, we don't consider it valid unless the GitHub CI checks all pass
        green."
      </p>
      <p>
        Your agent sends the memory to Weft, with provenance about the conversation it came from.
        It looks like this:
      </p>
      <pre><code>content: "When pushing a PR to GitHub, do not tell the user the PR is valid unless all CI checks come back green"
type: "preference"
topic: ["pull-requests", "ci"]
confidence: 0.97
source: "conversation"</code></pre>
      <p>
        Then, the memory is persisted, converted to a semantic vector, and available for your
        agents. The next time it goes to push a PR, the instruction surfaces.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/how-it-works">See how it works →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="benchmarks">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Weft Works In Ways That Are Hard To Benchmark</h2>
      <p>
        Weft doesn't benchmark well because it doesn't work the way most benchmarks test. Most
        "agent memory" benchmarks assume the system remembers everything and so they're designed
        to see how well a system can sift through hundreds of conversations to find the right
        answer. Weft intentionally doesn't do this.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, it remembers the things you tell it to, and not a lot else. I want Weft to have
        an auto-memory function, but I don't want a gimmick. I want it to remember the stuff you'd
        tell it to remember without burying it with a ton of information you don't care about.
        Weft will never tell you how many times you told your system to "make no mistakes" —
        unless you tell it to.
      </p>
      <p>
        For transparency, we ran it through
        <a href="https://github.com/xiaowu0162/LongMemEval" rel="noopener">LongMemEval</a>-S
        (multi-session chat memory, ~40 sessions per question). We ran the full 500-question split
        using <code>gpt-6-luna</code> via the API, in "turn tier" mode — the version of Weft that
        aligns most closely with how LongMemEval works.
      </p>
      <p>
        Judged accuracy was <strong>67.54%</strong> as scored by LongMemEval's official judge —
        one paid run, nothing hidden. The weakest question types (multi-session,
        knowledge-update, single-session preference) are the first post-launch improvements Weft
        will ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        But the benchmark measures the system that ships today, configured the way you actually
        use it, not in a harness designed to make the numbers higher than they really are.
      </p>
      <p>
        Full results, per-type breakdown, cost ledger, and the harness to reproduce it — all in
        the repo:
        <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/docs/benchmarks.md" rel="noopener"
          >docs/benchmarks.md</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>5 Minutes To A Better Memory</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Install Weft, run <code>weft up</code>, register the MCP server, save your first memory.
        Then every agent you use, in any harness, can remember the things you care about.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/getting-started">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/release-notes">Release notes</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
